aper could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference (up to top 2.5% papers)\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle: the paper needs substantial revisions that can be completed by the next ARR cycle\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle: the paper needs substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit: the paper has to be fully redone, or it is not relevant to the *ACL community (e.g., it is in no way related to computational processing of language)\n\n## Assessment Criteria\n\nThe overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of the paper if committed to an *ACL venue, reflecting assessment of soundness, excitement, and other factors like novelty and impact."],"durationSeconds":7.4389201149999975}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012ori5VwRqUgvuLsPucp3C9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the ARR/EMNLP review form scale. Let me also get the soundness and excitement scales. Let me compile what I know:\n\n**Overall Assessment (1-5, with half points):**\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Paper is largely complete, minor points\n- 3 = Major points that may be revised\n- 2 = Needs significant revisions / Resubmit next cycle\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle\n\n\n- 1 = Do not resubmit / not publishable at *ACL\n\n**Soundness (1-5):**\n- 5 = Paper is largely complete, no clear points of revision\n- 4 = Minor points\n- 3 = Major points\n- 2 = Significant revisions needed\n- 1 = Not likely publishable even after revisions\n\n**Excitement (1-5):** follows a similar scale\n\nNow I'll set up the review process with two specialized reviewers—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—then run through a structured roundtable discussion moving from independent assessments to cross-sharing to consensus-building to final synthesis.\n\nI'm dispatching both reviewers to work in parallel.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":561,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1698,"cache_read_input_tokens":61130,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1698},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b35164d6-59ff-4dc9-a0a2-e7bd8feeacab","uuid":"be77df6b-621e-48b9-8248-0f38aaaca0ee"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012ori5VwRqUgvuLsPucp3C9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the EMNLP/ARR review standards. Now dispatching two reviewers in parallel: R1 (Methods + Experiments) and R2 (Novelty + Related Work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":561,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1698,"cache_read_input_tokens":61130,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1698},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b35164d6-59ff-4dc9-a0a2-e7bd8feeacab","uuid":"22b396f1-f0a6-4ff5-964b-0f697e9b5a87"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012ori5VwRqUgvuLsPucp3C9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GXZppFwTASjrABhJ2fF4BU","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus area is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ACL Rolling Review)\n\n**Scoring scale (1–5, half-point increments):**\n- 5 = Award-worthy (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Largely complete, minor revisions\n- 3 = Major revisions needed\n- 2 = Significant revisions / resubmit\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle\n- 1 = Not publishable at *ACL\n\n**Three dimensions:** Soundness (same 1–5), Excitement (same 1–5), Overall Assessment.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"OntoCoDec: Near-Complete Type-Valid Information Extraction via Compiled Ontology Constraints\"\n\nThe paper addresses ontology constraint violations in LLM-based zero-shot information extraction. It:\n1. Conducts a **violation landscape study** measuring domain/range (DR), relation vocabulary (RV), entity type (ET), and cross-field consistency (CF) violations on SciERC (7 relations) and DocRED (96 relations) with Qwen3-8B under zero-shot prompting.\n2. Proposes **OntoCoDec**, a framework that compiles IE ontology schemas into two composable constrained-decoding layers:\n   - Grammar layer (CFG via XGrammar): enforces JSON structure, relation vocabulary, entity-type vocabulary\n   - Semantic layer (TypeConstraintProcessor): stateful entity registry + dynamic logit masking for domain/range constraints\n3. Layers compose via bitwise AND of token masks; fallback to grammar-only when intersection is empty.\n4. Two decoding strategies: S1 (single-pass) and S2 (two-pass).\n\n## Key Results (from paper)\n\n**SciERC ablation (Qwen3-8B, Table 2):**\n| Config | DR | RV | ET | CF | R-F1 | E-F1 | Triples |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Unconstrained | 15.4 | 1.4 | 0.4 | 0.1 | 8.23 | 31.01 | 1724 |\n| Grammar-only | 14.8 | 0.0 | 0.0 | 0.1 | 8.23 | 30.98 | — |\n| Semantic-only | 11.7 | 1.5 | 0.4 | 2.7 | 8.15 | 31.01 | — |\n| Full S1 | 0.9 | 0.0 | 0.0 | 9.9 | 8.36 | 30.98 | 1729 |\n| Full S2 | 1.0 | 0.0 | 0.0 | 8.0 | 8.03 | 31.90 | 1644 |\n\n**DocRED ablation (Qwen3-8B, Table 3):**\n| Config | DR | RV | ET | CF | R-F1 | E-F1 | Triples |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Unconstrained | 18.5 | 29.7 | 2.5 | 0.1 | 7.74 | 58.15 | 17497 |\n| Grammar-only | 26.2 | 0.03 | 0.0 | 0.1 | 8.31 | 59.38 | — |\n| Semantic-only | 13.8 | 29.9 | 4.2 | 1.1 | 7.70 | 57.81 | — |\n| Full S1 | 10.2 | 0.03 | 0.0 | 2.6 | 8.23 | 59.37 | — |\n\n**Cross-model (Llama-3.1-8B, Table 4):**\n- SciERC: DR 13.3→0.1 (↓99.4%), CF=6.6%, R-F1 2.68→2.54\n- DocRED: DR 26.1→2.6 (↓90.2%), CF=12.0%, R-F1 5.98→6.27, parse failures 68→91 (6.8%→9.1%)\n\n**Post-hoc baseline (Table 5):**\n- SciERC: Post-hoc F1=8.63 > OntoCoDec F1=8.36; Post-hoc recall=10.68 < OntoCoDec recall=11.60\n- DocRED: Post-hoc F1=9.61 > OntoCoDec F1=8.23; Post-hoc rejects 48.1% of triples\n\n**Bootstrap significance (Table 6):**\n- SciERC R-F1: +0.13pp, p=0.58 (not significant)\n- DocRED R-F1: +0.50pp, p<0.001, CI [+0.23, +0.81]\n\n**S2 ablation:** DR=1.0%, CF=8.0%, R-F1=8.03 — CF reduction only 1.9pp vs S1, not significant.\n\n## Experiment Design Coverage\n\nThe paper covers experiments P001–P019 from the full plan. A P017 pilot (50 docs) was superseded by full S2. P020 was killed/superseded. All other major experiments are reported.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a **detailed review** covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2–3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (focus on method design, experimental rigor, ablation completeness)\n3. **Weaknesses** (focus on method effectiveness, experimental gaps, statistical concerns)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Preliminary Scores:**\n   - Soundness (1–5)\n   - Excitement (1–5)\n   - Overall Assessment (1–5)\n   - Confidence (1–5)\n\nBe thorough and critical. Assess whether the method's trade-offs (especially CF introduction, DocRED scalability gap, post-hoc comparison) are adequately addressed. Consider whether the zero-shot setting with ~8% R-F1 is a meaningful evaluation context. Evaluate the \"near-complete\" claim in the title against the DocRED results.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":561,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1698,"cache_read_input_tokens":61130,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1698},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b35164d6-59ff-4dc9-a0a2-e7bd8feeacab","uuid":"cc445666-ec18-480c-96c0-a2dc7694d918"}
